  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  version: \"4.11.0\"\n\npackage:\n  name: onioncat\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://github.com/rahra/onioncat/archive/refs/tags/v${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 75ff9eed332e97a9efb7999bbe48867d00e06ac20601cc72b87897d5b1859f99\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n  # OnionCat only builds on Windows through Cygwin, which conda-forge does not target.\n  skip: win\n  script:\n    - ./autogen.sh\n    - ./configure --prefix=${PREFIX} --host=${HOST} --build=${BUILD}\n    - make -j${CPU_COUNT}\n    - make install\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - ${{ compiler('c') }}\n    - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    - autoconf\n    - automake\n    - make\n\ntests:\n  - script:\n      # `ocat -h` prints the usage message and exits with a non-zero status,\n      # so match on the output instead of the exit code.\n      - ocat -h 2>&1 | grep -q \"usage:\"\n      # Round-trip an onion id through the OnionCat IPv6 mapping.\n      - ocat -i 5wyqrzbvrdsumnok.onion | grep -qx \"fd87:d87e:eb43:edb1:8e4:3588:e546:35ca\"\n      - ocat -o fd87:d87e:eb43:edb1:8e4:3588:e546:35ca | grep -qx \"5wyqrzbvrdsumnok.onion\"\n    requirements:\n      run:\n        - grep\n  - package_contents:\n      bin:\n        - ocat\n      files:\n        - share/man/man1/ocat.1\n        - share/doc/onioncat/README\n        - share/doc/onioncat/Garlicat-HOWTO\n      strict: true\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://www.onioncat.org/\n  repository: https://github.com/rahra/onioncat\n  documentation: https://github.com/rahra/onioncat/blob/master/README.md\n  license: GPL-3.0-only\n  license_file: COPYING\n  summary: A VPN adapter for Tor and I2P\n  description: |\n    OnionCat creates a transparent IPv6 layer on top of Tor's hidden services\n    or I2P's tunnels. It transmits any kind of IP-based data transparently\n    through the Tor/I2P network on a location hidden basis. You can think of it\n    as a peer-to-peer VPN between hidden services.\n\n    OnionCat is a stand-alone application which runs in userland and is a\n    connector between Tor/I2P and the local OS. Any protocol which is based on\n    IP can be transmitted, most notably TCP, UDP and ICMP.\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - pavelzw\n",
